1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,917
This is STAT 331, video 12. The topic is random vectors and the multivariate normal distribution.

2
00:00:09,367 --> 00:00:36,290
After this video, you can find the mean vector and the covariance matrix of a random vector. You can use and prove the rules for the mean and the variance of A times y. You can define the multivariate normal distribution and use its four properties. You can write multiple linear regression as a multivariate normal model. And you can solve the exercise and the two practice questions of Lecture 5.

3
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:49,220
In multiple linear regression, we have n responses, y 1 to y n. Each response is a random variable. We stack them in one column. This column is a random vector.

4
00:00:49,670 --> 00:00:57,115
Each entry has its own distribution. The entries can have different means. They can also depend on each other.

5
00:00:57,565 --> 00:01:10,088
We need two summaries of the whole vector. The first is the mean vector. The second is the covariance matrix. With these, one matrix equation does the work of n equations.

6
00:01:10,538 --> 00:01:22,250
Let y be a random vector with entries y 1 to y n. The T means transpose. So y is a column. Each curve shows the distribution of one entry.

7
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:28,737
Each entry has an expected value. The purple dot marks the mean of each distribution.

8
00:01:29,187 --> 00:01:37,209
The mean of the vector is the column of these means. We take the expectation entry by entry. We call this vector mu.

9
00:01:37,659 --> 00:01:50,437
The variance of a random vector is the expectation of y minus mu, times its transpose. y minus mu is a column, n by 1. Its transpose is a row, 1 by n.

10
00:01:50,887 --> 00:02:07,869
A column times a row gives an n by n matrix. Entry i, j is y i minus mu i, times y j minus mu j. On the diagonal, i equals j. There the expectation of the square is the variance of y i.

11
00:02:08,319 --> 00:02:20,991
Off the diagonal, the expectation of the product is the covariance of y i and y j. We call this matrix V. It is the covariance matrix, or the variance matrix.

12
00:02:21,441 --> 00:02:37,505
V is symmetric. Entry i, j equals entry j, i, because the covariance of y i and y j equals the covariance of y j and y i. The red boxes show entries 1, 3 and 3, 1.

13
00:02:37,955 --> 00:02:56,024
Here are samples of a random vector with two entries. Each dot is one draw of y 1 and y 2. Both variances are 1. The covariance is positive, 0.8. A large y 1 comes with a large y 2. The cloud tilts up.

14
00:02:56,474 --> 00:03:05,135
With covariance 0, the cloud has no tilt. The green ellipse is a circle here, because the two variances are equal.

15
00:03:05,585 --> 00:03:20,689
With covariance minus 0.8, a large y 1 comes with a small y 2. The cloud tilts down. So the diagonal of V gives the spread of each entry. The off-diagonal entries give the tilt.

16
00:03:21,139 --> 00:03:32,339
The slides give one more property. V is positive semi-definite. For every vector a in R n, a transpose V a is greater than or equal to 0.

17
00:03:32,789 --> 00:03:37,909
Here is the reason. Pick a direction a. The purple line shows that direction.

18
00:03:38,359 --> 00:03:48,621
Project each point onto the line. The projected value is a transpose y. It is one number for each draw. The histogram shows these numbers.

19
00:03:49,071 --> 00:04:04,687
Later in this video we show that the variance of a transpose y is a transpose V a. The orange curve has exactly this variance. A variance is never negative. So a transpose V a is never negative, for every a.

20
00:04:05,137 --> 00:04:22,438
Recall linear combinations of random variables. z is the sum of a i y i, plus a constant c. Its mean is the sum of a i times E of y i, plus c. In matrix form, E of z is a transpose mu, plus c.

21
00:04:22,888 --> 00:04:28,093
Let u be a second linear combination, with weights b i and a constant d.

22
00:04:28,543 --> 00:04:42,175
The covariance of z and u is a double sum. Each term is a i, times b j, times the covariance of y i and y j. The grid shows one term for each pair i, j.

23
00:04:42,625 --> 00:04:52,887
Add all n squared cells. In matrix notation, this sum is a transpose V b. The constants c and d do not change a covariance.

24
00:04:53,337 --> 00:05:04,174
Now three rules for a random vector y and a constant vector a. First, a constant does not change when you take its expectation. E of a is a.

25
00:05:04,624 --> 00:05:16,677
Second, E of a transpose y plus b is a transpose E of y, plus b. Here b is a constant. This is the matrix form of the rule for linear combinations.

26
00:05:17,127 --> 00:05:36,647
Third, the variance of a transpose y is a transpose, times the variance of y, times a. It is the covariance rule with b equal to a. Look at the shapes. A 1 by n row, times an n by n matrix, times an n by 1 column, gives a 1 by 1 result.

27
00:05:37,097 --> 00:05:43,668
So the result is one number. It is the variance of the single random variable a transpose y.

28
00:05:44,118 --> 00:06:02,123
Now take k linear combinations of y at the same time. z 1 is a 1 1 y 1, plus a 1 2 y 2, and so on, to a 1 n y n. The same holds for z 2 to z k. A is the k by n matrix of the weights a i j.

29
00:06:02,573 --> 00:06:10,275
Row 1 of A times y gives z 1. So the k equations are one matrix equation: z equals A y.

30
00:06:10,725 --> 00:06:16,293
Each row of A gives one entry of z. z is a random vector with k entries.

31
00:06:16,743 --> 00:06:29,820
Take the expectation of each entry. E of z i is the sum over j of a i j times E of y j. Stack these k results. E of z equals A times E of y.

32
00:06:30,270 --> 00:06:40,851
The variance rule is: the variance of z equals A, times the variance of y, times A transpose. The next two slides prove both rules.

33
00:06:41,301 --> 00:06:55,424
Here is the proof that E of A y equals A E of y. Write A y as a column. Entry i is the sum of a i j times y j. The expectation of a vector is taken entry by entry.

34
00:06:55,874 --> 00:07:11,405
Look at one entry. The weights a i j are constants. The expectation of a sum is the sum of the expectations. A constant comes out of the expectation. So each term becomes a i j times E of y j.

35
00:07:11,855 --> 00:07:18,148
Do this for every entry. Entry i becomes the sum of a i j times E of y j.

36
00:07:18,598 --> 00:07:26,342
This column is A times the column E of y. So E of A y equals A E of y. The proof is complete.

37
00:07:26,792 --> 00:07:33,555
Now the variance rule. Line 1 is the definition of the variance, with A y in place of y.

38
00:07:34,005 --> 00:07:45,973
Line 2. E of A y is A E of y, from the last slide. So A y minus E of A y equals A, times y minus E of y. We take A out on the left.

39
00:07:46,423 --> 00:08:03,127
Line 3. The transpose of a product reverses the order. The transpose of A times w is w transpose times A transpose. So the second factor becomes y minus E of y, transpose, times A transpose.

40
00:08:03,577 --> 00:08:17,017
Line 4. A and A transpose are constants. A comes out of the expectation on the left. A transpose comes out on the right. The order must stay the same, because matrix products do not commute.

41
00:08:17,467 --> 00:08:35,045
Line 5. The middle part is the definition of the variance of y. So the variance of A y is A, times the variance of y, times A transpose. The shapes give a k by k matrix, one row and one column for each entry of z.

42
00:08:35,495 --> 00:08:48,231
Now the multivariate normal distribution. Start with z, a vector of n independent standard normal variables. The cloud of z is round. It is centred at 0.

43
00:08:48,681 --> 00:08:55,785
Multiply by a constant matrix A. This stretches and turns the cloud. The ellipse shows the new shape.

44
00:08:56,235 --> 00:09:09,611
Add a constant vector mu. This moves the centre to mu. The result, y equals A z plus mu, has a multivariate normal distribution. We write: y follows M V N, mu, Sigma.

45
00:09:10,061 --> 00:09:18,744
Find the mean with our rules. E of y equals A times E of z, plus mu. E of z is 0. So E of y is mu.

46
00:09:19,194 --> 00:09:37,498
Find the variance. The variance of z is the identity matrix, because the entries are independent with variance 1. A constant shift does not change the variance. So the variance of y is A I A transpose, which is A A transpose. We call it Sigma.

47
00:09:37,948 --> 00:09:48,188
The slide gives the density of y. The bars around Sigma mean the determinant of Sigma. The purple dot marks mu. The density is highest there.

48
00:09:48,638 --> 00:10:04,190
The density depends on y only through one number: the quadratic form, y minus mu transpose, Sigma inverse, y minus mu. The green ellipses are the sets of points where this quadratic form is constant.

49
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:13,344
So the density is constant on each ellipse. The outer ellipses have a larger quadratic form. The density there is smaller.

50
00:10:13,794 --> 00:10:33,890
Check with n equal to 1. Sigma is the number sigma squared, and its determinant is sigma squared. The density becomes 1 over the square root of 2 pi sigma squared, times e to the power minus, y minus mu squared, over 2 sigma squared. This is the normal density.

51
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:46,095
Now four properties of the multivariate normal. Property 1 is linearity. Let u equal C y plus d, with a constant matrix C and a constant vector d.

52
00:10:46,545 --> 00:11:02,950
Put in y equals A z plus mu. Then u equals C A times z, plus C mu plus d. This has the same form as the definition: a constant matrix times z, plus a constant vector. So u is multivariate normal.

53
00:11:03,400 --> 00:11:12,808
The mean is C mu plus d. The variance is C Sigma C transpose. Both come from the rules for the mean and the variance of A y.

54
00:11:13,258 --> 00:11:25,055
Property 2 is about marginal distributions. Take a subset of the entries of y, for example only y 1. Then this subset is also multivariate normal.

55
00:11:25,505 --> 00:11:33,655
The bars show the values of y 1 alone, from the same cloud. The green curve is a normal density. It fits.

56
00:11:34,105 --> 00:11:44,601
The reason is linearity. To pick entries, multiply by a matrix of zeros and ones. Here y 1 equals the row 1, 0, times y.

57
00:11:45,051 --> 00:11:58,917
Property 3 is about conditional distributions. Split y into two parts, y 1 and y 2. Then fix y 2 at one value. Here we keep only the points with y 2 near 1.2.

58
00:11:59,367 --> 00:12:12,317
The distribution of y 1 among these points is normal again. So y 1 given y 2 is multivariate normal. The slides do not give the formulas for its mean and variance.

59
00:12:12,767 --> 00:12:26,441
Property 4 is about independence. Let y be multivariate normal. If Sigma i j is 0, then y i and y j are independent. The ellipse lies along the axes.

60
00:12:26,891 --> 00:12:34,230
If Sigma i j is not 0, the ellipse tilts. Then y 1 and y 2 depend on each other.

61
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:47,075
This property needs a multivariate normal vector. For other distributions, a covariance of 0 does not always give independence. The next exercise shows such a case.

62
00:12:47,525 --> 00:13:04,399
Here is the exercise from the slides. X 1 is standard normal. B is Bernoulli with probability one half, independent of X 1. The histogram shows a sample of X 1. The red curve is the N 0 1 density.

63
00:13:04,849 --> 00:13:15,644
Define X 2. If B is 0, X 2 equals X 1. If B is 1, X 2 equals minus X 1. So we flip the sign half of the time.

64
00:13:16,094 --> 00:13:26,505
The normal density is symmetric about 0. So a flip of the sign does not change the distribution. The histogram of X 2 has the same shape.

65
00:13:26,955 --> 00:13:38,624
The red curve fits X 2 too. So X 2 is standard normal. The slides check this with a simulation of one million draws. Here we use 4000 draws.

66
00:13:39,074 --> 00:13:45,239
Now let Y equal X 1 plus X 2. Is Y normal? Pause the video and try it.

67
00:13:45,689 --> 00:14:02,948
Look at the pairs X 1, X 2. When B is 0, X 2 equals X 1. These are the blue points, on the line with slope 1. When B is 1, X 2 equals minus X 1. These are the green points, on the line with slope minus 1.

68
00:14:03,398 --> 00:14:11,782
When B is 0, Y equals 2 X 1. When B is 1, Y equals X 1 minus X 1, which is 0.

69
00:14:12,232 --> 00:14:25,928
So the probability that Y equals 0 is the probability that B equals 1. This is one half. A normal variable has probability 0 at any single value. So Y is not normal.

70
00:14:26,378 --> 00:14:45,727
The reason: X 1 and X 2 are each normal, but the vector X 1, X 2 is not multivariate normal. No matrix A gives this vector as A times independent standard normals. A multivariate normal cloud is an ellipse. This cloud is two lines.

71
00:14:46,177 --> 00:15:06,615
One more check, added here. The covariance of X 1 and X 2 is E of X 1 squared, times E of 1 minus 2 B. This is 1 times 0, which is 0. But X 1 and X 2 are not independent. So the independence property needs a multivariate normal vector.

72
00:15:07,065 --> 00:15:27,715
Now the reason for this lecture. Multiple linear regression has p covariates. y i equals beta 0, plus beta 1 x i 1, and so on, to beta p x i p, plus epsilon i. The errors are I I D normal, with mean 0 and variance sigma squared.

73
00:15:28,165 --> 00:15:43,909
Stack the n equations. y is n by 1. X is n by p plus 1, with a first column of ones. Beta is p plus 1 by 1. Epsilon is n by 1. So y equals X beta plus epsilon.

74
00:15:44,359 --> 00:16:02,450
The errors are independent with the same variance. So the covariance matrix of epsilon has sigma squared on the diagonal, and 0 at all other positions. This is sigma squared I. So epsilon follows M V N, 0, sigma squared I.

75
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:14,100
Use linearity. y is epsilon plus the constant vector X beta. So y follows M V N, with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I.

76
00:16:14,550 --> 00:16:30,038
So each y i is normal, with mean x i transpose beta, where x i transpose is row i of X. All have variance sigma squared. They are independent, because the off-diagonal entries are 0.

77
00:16:30,488 --> 00:16:52,760
Practice question 1. y has three entries. Its mean is 1, 2, 2. Its variance matrix V is given. a is 1, 2, 3, and A is a 4 by 3 matrix. Compute four things: E of a transpose y, E of A y, the variance of a transpose y, and the variance of A y. Pause the video and try it.

78
00:16:53,210 --> 00:17:02,789
E of a transpose y is a transpose mu. This is 1 times 1, plus 2 times 2, plus 3 times 2. The answer is 11.

79
00:17:03,239 --> 00:17:10,428
E of A y is A mu. A is 4 by 3, and mu is 3 by 1. So the result is 4 by 1.

80
00:17:10,878 --> 00:17:29,523
Row 1: 1 minus 2, plus 0, which is minus 1. Row 2: 2 minus 2, which is 0. Row 3: 1 minus 2, which is minus 1. Row 4: 1 plus 2 plus 2, which is 5. So E of A y is minus 1, 0, minus 1, 5.

81
00:17:29,973 --> 00:17:43,648
The variance of a transpose y is a transpose V a. First compute V a. Row 1 of V times a: 4 times 1, plus 0 times 2, plus 1 times 3. This is 7.

82
00:17:44,098 --> 00:17:51,287
Row 2 of V times a: 0, plus 3 times 2, plus minus 1 times 3. This is 3.

83
00:17:51,737 --> 00:18:00,377
Row 3 of V times a: 1, minus 2, plus 4 times 3. This is 11. So V a is 7, 3, 11.

84
00:18:00,827 --> 00:18:16,358
Now a transpose times V a. 1 times 7, plus 2 times 3, plus 3 times 11. This is 7 plus 6 plus 33, which is 46. So the variance of a transpose y is 46.

85
00:18:16,808 --> 00:18:35,496
The variance of A y is A V A transpose. First compute A V. It is 4 by 3. Row 1 of A is 1, minus 1, 0. So row 1 of A V is row 1 of V minus row 2 of V: 4, minus 3, 2. The other rows follow in the same way.

86
00:18:35,946 --> 00:18:54,101
Then multiply A V by A transpose. The result is 4 by 4. First the diagonal entries. Entry 1, 1 is row 1 of A V times row 1 of A: 4, plus 3, plus 0, which is 7. The diagonal is 7, 9, 6, 11.

87
00:18:54,551 --> 00:19:09,335
Then the entries off the diagonal. For example, entry 1, 2 is row 1 of A V times row 2 of A: 4 times 0, minus 3 times 1, plus 2 times minus 1. This is minus 5.

88
00:19:09,785 --> 00:19:23,417
Check the result. It is symmetric: entry 1, 2 and entry 2, 1 are both minus 5. The diagonal entries are positive, because they are the variances of the four entries of A y.

89
00:19:23,867 --> 00:19:47,056
Practice question 2. x has mean mu and variance V. y equals A x, with A p by n. w equals B x, with B q by n. C is the cross-covariance matrix. Entry i, j is the covariance of y i and w j. Write C in terms of mu, V, A and B. Pause the video and try it.

90
00:19:47,506 --> 00:20:02,013
Stack y and w into one vector. Its variance matrix has four blocks. The diagonal blocks are the variance of y and the variance of w. C is the top right block. It is p by q.

91
00:20:02,463 --> 00:20:21,407
Write C as an expectation: E of y minus E of y, times w minus E of w, transpose. By the mean rule, y minus E of y is A times x minus mu. In the same way, w minus E of w is B times x minus mu.

92
00:20:21,857 --> 00:20:45,153
Put these in. The transpose of B times x minus mu is x minus mu transpose, times B transpose. A and B transpose are constants, so they come out of the expectation. The middle part is V. So C equals A V B transpose. The shapes check: p by n, times n by n, times n by q, gives p by q.

93
00:20:45,603 --> 00:20:50,851
C does not depend on mu. A shift of the mean does not change a covariance.

94
00:20:51,301 --> 00:21:00,453
To recap. The mean vector is the expectation entry by entry. The covariance matrix is symmetric and positive semi-definite.

95
00:21:00,903 --> 00:21:08,263
For a constant matrix A, E of A y is A E of y, and the variance of A y is A V A transpose.

96
00:21:08,713 --> 00:21:26,633
A multivariate normal vector is A z plus mu, with independent standard normal z. Its variance is A A transpose. Linear functions, marginals and conditionals of it are multivariate normal. A zero covariance gives independence.

97
00:21:27,083 --> 00:21:45,579
Entries that are each normal do not always make a multivariate normal vector. Multiple linear regression says y is M V N with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I. The next video uses this model to estimate beta by least squares.
